    "editorial:Content_en.txt": {
      "fingerprint": "58a4be635ae296d9c605634579ff389e645f6a4694a97a7621875f8ceaf242bc",
      "kind": "editorial",
      "status": "blocked",
      "issues": [
        "- The Type A substitution method incorrectly interprets \"X is called Y\" as \"code word X = real Y\", but in standard exam usage (SSC, Railway, Bank) the phrase means \"real direction X is renamed Y in the code\", so the decoding table must map code Y → real X. This inversion error makes the introductory example (walking towards coded 'North' actually walks East, should be West) and the solved example (walking towards coded 'South' actually walks East, should be West; final answer North-East, should be South-West) both wrong."
      ],
